#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec44686a-601b-52b3-81df-e57e6708fb93" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Multiplicative Property"}] :question/id #uuid "53185b25-c690-500a-9a82-c18b37668e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-6106" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} xy\\ \\!e^{x+y}\\ \\textrm d A$ over the rectangle $R= [0,1] \\times [1,2]$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\iint\\limits_{R} xy \\\\ e^{x+y}\\: \\textrm d A &= \\iint\\limits_{R} \\Big(x\\ \\! e^x \\Big)\\Big(y\\ \\! e^{y} \\Big) \\textrm d A \\\\[5pt] &= \\left(\\int_0^1x \\\\ e^{x} \\ \\textrm d x\\right)\\left(\\int_1^2 y \\ \\!e^{y} \\ \\textrm d y\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\Big[x\\ \\!e^x-e^x\\Big]_0^1 \\cdot \\Big[y\\ \\!e^y-e^y\\Big]_1^2 \\\\[5pt] &= \\Big(e^1-e^1 - \\left(0-e^0\\right)\\Big) \\cdot \\Big(2e^2-e^2 - \\left(e^1-e^1\\right) \\Big) \\\\[5pt] &= 1\\cdot e^2 \\\\[5pt] &= e^2 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "a5567cb9-1c16-56a6-b0e8-759edf693a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6107" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} \\left(x^2-y^2 +3\\right) \\textrm d A$, where $R= [-1,3] \\times [2, 4]$." :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(R)(x^{2} - y^{2} + 3)dA &= ∬_(R)x^{2}dA - ∬_(R)y^{2}dA + ∬_(R)3dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each of the three integrals.\n\n- The function under the first integral is independent of $y$. So, we have\n$∬_(R)x^{2}dA|= (4 - 2)∫_{-1}^{3}x^{2}dx; = 2 \\cdot \\frac{x^{3}}{3} \\mid _{-1}^{3}; = 2(\\frac{27}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}); = \\frac{56}{3}$.\n- The function under the second integral is independent of $x$. So, we have\n$∬_(R)y^{2}dA|= (3 - (-1))∫_{2}^{4}y^{2}dy; = 4∫_{2}^{4}y^{2}dy; = 4 \\cdot \\frac{y^{3}}{3} \\mid _{2}^{4}; = \\frac{4}{3}4^{3} - \\frac{4}{3}2^{3}; = \\frac{224}{3}$.\n- The function under the third integral is a constant, so we have\n$∬_(R)3dA = 3Area(R) = 3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 4) = 24$.\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n∬_(R)(x^{2} - y^{2} + 3)dA = \\frac{56}{3} - \\frac{224}{3} + 24 =-32\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "0cb8fa8a-5be1-5f53-bfde-5acf325f6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-6108" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, where\n\n${2,\\begin{vmatrix}if0 < x < 2,-1 < y < 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{xy + x + y + 1},\\end{vmatrix}if0 < x < 2, 0 < y < 1$,\nand $R$ is the domain of $f$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have\n\n$$\nR = R_1 \\cup R_2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nR_1= (0,2) \\times (-1,0)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nR_2= (0,2) \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\iint\\limits_{R} f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A &= \\iint\\limits_{R_1} 2 \\ \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{R_2} \\dfrac{1}{xy+x+y+1} \\ \\textrm d A \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\iint\\limits_{R_1} \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{R_2} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)(y+1)} \\ \\textrm d A \\\\[5pt] &= 2(2-0)(0-(-1)) + \\Bigg(\\int_0^2 \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\ \\textrm d x \\Bigg) \\Bigg(\\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{y+1}\\ \\textrm d y \\Bigg) \\\\[5pt] &= 4 + \\Big[\\ln(x+1)\\Big]_0^2 \\cdot \\Big[\\ln(y+1)\\Big]_0^1 \\\\[5pt] &= 4+ \\ln 3\\ln 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128942 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8505beff-75b8-57bf-9312-e0fcf32f44fd" :knowledge-point/title "Using Additivity of Double Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "1c227b12-d40b-53a8-b568-b7bd380acbee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7023" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n$\\iint\\limits_{C} f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = 8, \\qquad \\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = -5$,\nfind the value of\n$\\iint\\limits_{C \\cup D} 3f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A$." :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(C∪D)3f(x, y)dA &= 3∬_(C∪D)f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= 3(∬_(C)f(x, y)dA + ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA) \\\\\n&= 3(8 + (-5)) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec44686a-601b-52b3-81df-e57e6708fb93" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Multiplicative Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fece8aa-41df-52a5-8c21-d1ef3d25a395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128487} {:answer/id #uuid "a20ce637-1626-5222-8e33-7802082782b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128488} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4c985e-7cdb-5913-bc8b-2dc1f996dd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128489} {:answer/id #uuid "515a563d-b414-5e28-95bf-e95e519da264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(1 - e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128490} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6efdf4-2d6d-5cb5-8f5d-363e91370ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6efdf4-2d6d-5cb5-8f5d-363e91370ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128491} :answer-fi